## Before class

1. **Open Source Guides — [*Best Practices for Maintainers*](https://opensource.guide/best-practices/)** — **the primary reading.** Documenting your processes, saying what you expect, learning to say no. It's written for people running established projects, and nearly all of it applies on day one of yours. Its central idea frames the whole session: writing things down is how you scale yourself.
2. **Mozilla — [*Bug Writing Guidelines*](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/page.cgi?id=bug-writing.html)** — the classic description of a bug report a stranger can act on: one failure, steps to reproduce, expected versus actual. Some details are specific to Firefox; the anatomy isn't. We'll hold real reports up against it in class.
3. **Look at your own repository as a stranger would.** Open it in a private browser window, logged out, and give it ninety seconds. Can you tell what it is? Could you run it? May you use it? Note what would stop you.
4. **Bring a laptop**, and your Session 3 license lean (from your <course-link type="assignment" id="license-oss">License / Open-Source Short Response</course-link>). Your project decides its license in class.

