## Purpose

Every project has a front door: the handful of files a stranger reads, in roughly the same order, before deciding whether to stay. *What is this? Can I run it? May I use it? How do I help? What's it like here? Where's it going?* A potential contributor, a user evaluating your tool, an employer looking at your GitHub profile: all of them ask those questions, and most give a project about ninety seconds to answer them.

The answers are files. A **README** that says what the project is and gets a newcomer running. A **LICENSE** that says what they may do with it. A **CONTRIBUTING** guide that says how to help. A **code of conduct** that sets the tone. And the **issue and pull request templates** that shape every report and every change the project will ever receive. None of them is hard to write. All of them are easy to leave out, and a project that leaves them out turns strangers away without ever knowing it did.

In Session 3 you learned why governance matters and how licenses differ. In Session 13 you set your project's workflow. This week all of it becomes **files in your repository**: today you see what a good front door looks like and decide what yours needs, and next class you build it.

The other half of today is the **issue**: the bug report, the feature request. As a founder you don't only write issues. You **design the norms** every future issue follows, because whatever your templates ask for is what you'll get.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this session, you'll be able to:

1. **Audit a repository's front door** the way a stranger would: what they need, in what order, and what's missing.
2. **Write a README that onboards**: what the project is and who it's for, how to run it (or, until it runs, an honest status), how to help.
3. **Choose your project's license** and justify the choice in a paragraph, for this project's goals.
4. **Distinguish an error, a defect, a fault, and a failure**, and write a bug report a stranger can act on: steps to reproduce, expected versus actual, environment, evidence.
5. **Design issue and pull request templates** that make a good report the easy one to write.

---

## What we'll do together

**Two front doors, walked live.** A strong one and a weak one, both real: [Textualize/rich](https://github.com/Textualize/rich), a widely used Python library, and [dagorym/SSW](https://github.com/dagorym/SSW), a game project of your instructor's that has real code, real history, and almost no front door. We'll ask the stranger's questions of each, in order, and notice how much of what makes the strong one work cost its maintainer an hour or less.

**A license clinic.** Your Session 3 response was individual and provisional. Today your project decides for real: who should be able to use it, whether you want a patent grant, what your dependencies require. You'll leave with the license and a paragraph on why it fits. *(The <course-link type="page" id="pg-license-cheat-sheet">License Cheat Sheet</course-link> will be open.)*

**The anatomy of a good issue.** Four words that make bug reports precise, then real reports from repositories your instructor works in, from weakest to strongest, held up against the anatomy from the reading. You'll rewrite a weak one in pairs. Then you'll reread the starter issues your project filed in the Session 14 lab, written before any of this was taught, and fix the weakest one.

**Your own repository.** With the printed CP7 Checklist, you'll audit your repository as a stranger would and write a punch list in the order you'll fix things. Then you'll sketch your bug template's fields, your pull request template's checklist, and the labels you'll start with. Before you leave, you'll write the first sentence of your README. It's the hardest sentence of the week.

---

## What you'll leave with

- **Your project's license**, chosen, with the paragraph that justifies it.
- **A punch list** for your repository's front door, in the order you'll fix it. It's the agenda for next class.
- **Sketches of your templates and labels**, ready to build.
- **The first sentence of your README.** Expect to rewrite it at least once.
- **A working definition of a good issue**, and at least one of your project's issues rewritten to meet it.

